methods.description:
Create or replace a procedural Three.js code model. Use it whenever the scene needs a recognizable object the prop catalog lacks (a campfire, a guitar, a piano…) — instead of repurposing a semantically different prop or assembling one object from several props. args: { code, id?, label?, position?, referencePath? }.
- code (required): the COMPLETE plain-JavaScript program; no patch mode, import/require/await/fetch/DOM, or external dependencies. Minimal valid contract:
```js
function createModel(ctx) {
  const { THREE } = ctx;
  const root = new THREE.Group();
  const body = new THREE.Group(); body.name = "body";
  body.add(new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1), new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x888888 })));
  root.add(body);
  return root;
}
```
Return a THREE.Group (or { root }). Named top-level children become editable `parts`; use 4–15 semantic names, ground-origin meters, facing +Z, and natural pivots. Each semantic assembly (wheel, frame, handlebars…) must be its OWN top-level child — wrapping everything in one container group is rejected at validation (≥8 meshes with ≤1 named part), because it disables per-part layout verification and part editing. Models are static; do not write an update hook. Numbers fed to geometry constructors and transforms must be finite: call methods like `v.length()` (never pass the bare method `v.length`), and read the length before `normalize()` — it mutates in place. Any NaN vertex/transform fails validation.

TIERS — pick before writing code:
- Scene-dressing prop (supporting object judged within the whole shot — a campfire behind the actors, a guitar at medium distance): ONE call building a rough shape from a few primitives. Get proportions, silhouette, and ground contact right; resolve the returned warnings; then let the regular scene.snapshot review judge it. Skip model.capture/model.compare and refine loops unless the scene image shows it broken (then at most one fix pass).
- Hero object (close-up subject, reference image provided, or the user asks for fidelity): the staged BUILD PROTOCOL below is mandatory when it has ≥6 parts or strong spatial constraints — vehicles, furniture, machines. Simple hero objects (a crate, a barrel) may still be built in one call.

BUILD PROTOCOL (hero objects):
1. Write a part layout spec BEFORE any code: per part — approximate primitive, size (m), center position, orientation axis, and which part it attaches to. Load the `director-stage` skill’s object-construction reference for the spec format and layout axioms.
2. First model.generate call is a BLOCKOUT: one simple primitive per part, correct sizes/positions/axes only, no detail. Verify layout via partDetails + model.capture before adding any detail; a failed layout check means fixing the BLOCKOUT, never proceeding.
3. Refine by passing the SAME id (replace in place, transform preserved). Do not create a second model and delete the blockout — that bypasses the replace contract and loses the verified transform.

- id: omit to create; pass an exact/unique-prefix code-model id to replace in place, preserving its transform and still-valid part overrides.
- label: display name ≤40 chars. position: {x,y,z} meters; omitted new models auto-place. referencePath: workspace reference-image path.
Returns { ok, id, label, replaced, parts[], partDetails[], bbox, stats, warnings[], revision }.
- partDetails[]: per-part AABB { name, center, size, meshes } in model space. Immediately cross-check RELATIVE relations, not just absolute axes (an absolute rule like "wheels thin in X" is satisfied by a whole model rotated 90° and is therefore worthless alone):
  1. travel axis = the front→rear wheel center line; it must run along Z (facing +Z);
  2. every wheel/disc part's THINNEST size axis must be PERPENDICULAR to the travel axis (thin axis ∥ travel = wheel mounted sideways, cannot roll);
  3. mirrored parts have mirrored centers; attached parts have touching boxes.
- warnings[] now includes deterministic findings: `disconnected` (a part's box touches no other part), `longest horizontal span runs along X` (violates facing +Z), and `mounted sideways` (a wheel-like part's rotation axis parallel to the model's length axis). Each one must be fixed or explicitly justified before continuing to detail or reporting completion — never summarize a model with active warnings as "no errors".
Inspect bbox/partDetails/warnings immediately. Compile/runtime failures use stable error codes and a phase-tagged message; fix the code, never retry unchanged. Success only proves construction. For a hero build, load the `director-stage` skill’s object-construction reference, then use model.capture and actually inspect its images before claiming fidelity; a scene-dressing prop is judged in the normal whole-scene snapshot instead.
